findings.body:
The setter for `RetainedFileCountLimit` was changed from `value <= 0` to `value < 0`. The new XML doc says "Gets or sets a strictly positive value representing the maximum retained file count," and the exception text still says "must be positive." Both now disagree with the code, which accepts 0.

This matters at runtime. `FileLoggerProvider` only prunes old log files when `_maxRetainedFiles > 0`, so a 0 value silently turns off retention cleanup. Log files then accumulate on the App Service disk until the disk fills up. Before this PR, the same input threw immediately with a clear error.

This is a behavior change inside a PR titled and described as documentation only. There is also no test covering the 0 boundary.

::diff{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/AzureFileLoggerOptions.cs" lines="48-58"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Whether 0 should mean "keep none" or "no limit" is an API decision that should be made on purpose. As written, it is neither documented nor tested.

The PR should revert the operator to `value <= 0`, matching the doc and the message, and the doc should keep saying "strictly positive."

::suggestion{file="src/Logging.AzureAppServices/src/AzureFileLoggerOptions.cs" lines="53-53"}
            if (value <= 0)

The `TryGetValidatableParameterInfo` signature now ends with `) {` on the same line. The rest of this file, and the rest of the repo, put the opening brace on its own line. The `TryGetValidatableTypeInfo` method above it, and the other methods in the same file, still follow that convention.

This is a one-character cosmetic regression, but it sits in a file the PR is otherwise reformatting, so it is easy to fix while the author is in there.

